    {
      "id": "rich-text-editor", "icon": "PenLine", "name": "RichTextEditor", "category": "Inputs", "summary": "Lets the user write formatted text, saved as sanitized HTML.", "keywords": ["wysiwyg", "html editor", "rich text", "text editor", "editor", "formatted text", "checklist", "task list"],
      "features": [
        { "feature": "Label, hint and errors from the model" },
        { "feature": "Posts in a plain form; sanitized on the server" },
        { "feature": "Toolbar, shortcuts and paste", "noscript": "none" }
      ],
      "examples": [
        { "id": "rich-text-editor-announcement", "title": "In a form", "lead": "A RichTextEditor's value is HTML: headings, lists, quotes, code, bold, italic and the like, with no styles or classes. It's sanitized on the server whenever it binds, so the model only ever holds safe HTML. To show it, use `RichText`.",
          "notes": {
            "noscript": "The field is a text box holding the HTML. It still posts, and is sanitized.",
            "static": "IzzyBlazor's script makes the text editable and keeps the field's value current as the user types, so it posts with the form."
          } },
        { "id": "rich-text-editor-formats", "title": "Everything it formats", "lead": "The default tools format headings and paragraphs, with bold, italic, underline, strikethrough and inline code. They also make numbered and bulleted lists nested in each other, quotes, code blocks and horizontal lines.", "after": ["The text-style menu offers normal text and headings 1 to 5. Quote and code block are tools of their own.", "When the text shows under a page's own heading 1, set `TopHeading` to 2, and the menu starts there (see Styling the content)."] },
        { "id": "rich-text-editor-extended", "title": "Layout tools", "lead": "For pages people lay out, such as newsletters and articles, set `Tools` to `RichTextTools.Extended`. It adds alignment, subscript and superscript, text color and highlight, sizes, and fonts from `Fonts`.", "after": ["The colors come from a palette that reads in light and dark themes. Sizes come in steps that scale with the text around them, or as fixed sizes from `FontSizes`.", "The value stores them as the library's own classes, plus a font from your list, so it still carries no free styles. `RichText` shows them when it has the same `Fonts`."] },
        { "id": "rich-text-editor-custom-colors", "title": "Custom colors", "lead": "To allow any color, such as a brand's, set `CustomColors`. The text color and highlight menus then end with \"Custom…\", which opens a color panel.", "after": ["The color is stored as a style with six hex digits. The sanitizer keeps that style only with the option, so set `CustomColors` on `RichText` too.", "The option is off by default because a color chosen in a light theme can be unreadable in a dark one. The palette's colors avoid that."] },
        { "id": "rich-text-editor-comment", "title": "A short comment", "lead": "For short notes and comments, set `Tools` to `RichTextTools.Basic`: bold, italic, links and the two lists. `Rows` sets the height the text starts at, and `Placeholder` the prompt in it while it's empty." },
        { "id": "rich-text-editor-length", "title": "Limiting the length", "lead": "To limit the text, set `MaxLength`; it counts the characters readers see, not the HTML around them, and is checked on the server. To show the count under the field, set `ShowCount`. Don't use `[StringLength]` on the property: it would count the HTML.",
          "notes": {
            "noscript": "The count shows the length the page loaded with. A text over the limit gets its message after posting.",
            "static": "IzzyBlazor's script keeps the count current as the user types and marks it once it passes the limit."
          } },
        { "id": "rich-text-editor-states", "title": "Read-only and disabled", "lead": "A read-only editor has no toolbar; the text can be selected and still posts. A disabled one doesn't post." }
      ],
      "topics": [
        {
          "id": "toolbar", "title": "Toolbar and shortcuts", "summary": "Choose the tools in the toolbar. Shortcuts work for every format, shown or not.", "members": ["Tools", "RichTextTools", "WrapToolbar", "ToolbarContent", "InsertHtmlAsync"],
          "intro": [
            "`RichTextTools.Default` shows every tool and `RichTextTools.Basic` four of them. To choose your own, combine tools with `|`. `RichTextTools.None` hides the toolbar and leaves the shortcuts. When the toolbar is too wide it wraps onto more rows, filling each; to keep it one row, with what doesn't fit in a … menu at its end, set `WrapToolbar` to false.",
            "With nothing selected, a format's shortcut or tool turns it on for what's typed next, and again turns it off.",
            "Shortcuts: Ctrl+B bold, Ctrl+I italic, Ctrl+U underline, Ctrl+Shift+X strikethrough, Ctrl+= subscript, Ctrl+Shift+= superscript, Ctrl+Shift+L, E and R align start, center and end, Ctrl+E inline code, Ctrl+K link, Ctrl+Shift+8 bulleted list, Ctrl+Shift+7 numbered list, Ctrl+] and Ctrl+[ indent and outdent a list item, Ctrl+Alt+0 paragraph, Ctrl+Alt+2 to 4 headings, Ctrl+Z undo, Ctrl+Y redo, Ctrl+Shift+V paste as plain text. On a Mac, ⌘ takes Ctrl's place. Alt+F10 moves to the toolbar and Escape back to the text."
          ],
          "examples": [
            { "id": "rich-text-editor-tools", "title": "Choosing tools", "lead": "These release notes leave out underline, which reads as a link, and the rule. Set `Tools` to the ones you want." },
            { "id": "rich-text-editor-one-row", "title": "One row", "lead": "With `WrapToolbar` false, the toolbar stays one row, and the tools that don't fit move into a … menu at its end, with the same choices." },
            { "id": "rich-text-editor-signature", "title": "A tool of your own", "modes": ["interactive"], "lead": "To add your own tools, put them in `ToolbarContent`; they go at the end of the toolbar. `InsertHtmlAsync` puts HTML at the caret, sanitized, and Ctrl+Z undoes it. It works on interactive pages." }
          ]
        },
        {
          "id": "security", "title": "Security", "summary": "What the editor's value can hold, where it's sanitized, and how to show it safely.", "members": ["RichText"],
          "intro": [
            "The editor's value is sanitized on the server by `RichTextSanitizer` every time it binds, from a post or on an interactive page. Anyone can post to a form, so the server never trusts what the browser sends.",
            "The sanitizer writes a new document from what it allows: paragraphs, headings, lists, quotes, code, rules, bold, italic, underline, strikethrough, links, images and tables. `href` and `src` are limited to web, mail, phone and relative URLs. Scripts, event handlers and `javascript:` links never get through.",
            "Styles and classes are dropped, except the editor's own: its classes for alignment, color, highlight and size, a font or size from your `Fonts` and `FontSizes`, and, with `CustomColors`, a color or highlight as six hex digits.",
            "Pasted text goes through the same list, so a paste from Word or a web page keeps its headings, lists and bold, and drops its fonts, colors and classes. Ctrl+Shift+V pastes plain text.",
            "Show saved HTML with `RichText`, which sanitizes it again as it renders, so HTML saved before, or written by another system, is safe too. To clean HTML yourself, call `RichTextSanitizer.Sanitize`. A Content Security Policy that blocks inline script adds a second line of defense."
          ],
          "examples": []
        },
        {
          "id": "markdown", "title": "Markdown shortcuts", "summary": "Typed Markdown becomes formats as you type, for people who write it out of habit.", "members": ["MarkdownShortcuts"],
          "intro": [
            "At the start of a line, `## `, `### ` and `#### ` make headings, `- ` or `* ` a bulleted list, `1. ` a numbered list, `> ` a quote, and three backticks and a space a code block. `---` alone on a line, then Enter, makes a horizontal line. In a line, `**bold**`, `*italic*` and `` `code` `` turn into their formats as the closing mark is typed. Ctrl+Z undoes a change.",
            "The value is still HTML: Markdown is only a way of typing. To turn the shortcuts off, set `MarkdownShortcuts` to false."
          ],
          "examples": [
            { "id": "rich-text-editor-markdown", "title": "Typing Markdown", "lead": "Type `## ` and a space for a heading, `- ` and a space for a list, or `**text**` for bold." }
          ]
        },
        {
          "id": "links", "title": "Links", "summary": "Add, change and remove links, and open them in a new tab.", "members": ["RichTextTools"],
          "intro": [
            "The link tool, or Ctrl+K, opens a dialog: the address, the link's text (the selected text, which a new text replaces), and whether it opens in a new tab. With the caret in a link, the same tool changes it or removes it, keeping its text. Escape or Cancel closes the dialog and puts focus back in the text, with its selection.",
            "Addresses can be web (`https:`, `http:`), mail (`mailto:`), phone (`tel:`) or relative. Any other scheme, such as `javascript:`, is refused in the dialog and removed by the sanitizer. A link that opens a new tab gets `rel=\"noopener noreferrer\"`."
          ],
          "examples": []
        },
        {
          "id": "images", "title": "Images", "summary": "Add images by address, upload, paste or drop, each with a description.", "members": ["ImageUploadUrl", "AllowDataImages", "MaxImageSize"],
          "intro": [
            "The image tool opens a dialog with the image's source, from your device or the web, its size, and its description: the alternative text screen readers read in its place. The size keeps the image's proportions. An image that only decorates can be marked decorative, with an empty description.",
            "The dialog opens on its own after an image is pasted or dropped, so every image gets a description. Clicking an image selects it, and the tool then changes or removes it.",
            "To let people upload images, map an endpoint in your app and set `ImageUploadUrl` to it. The editor posts each chosen, pasted or dropped image there as multipart form data, in a field named `file`, with the form's antiforgery token. The endpoint stores it and answers with its URL, as text or as JSON `{ \"url\": \"...\" }`; the editor puts that URL in the value. Check the file's type and size on the server too.",
            "With no endpoint, `AllowDataImages` embeds images in the value as `data:` URLs instead, which needs no storage but makes the value as large as its images. `MaxImageSize` limits both (2 MB by default). PNG, JPEG, GIF, WebP and AVIF are taken; SVG never is, since it can carry script."
          ],
          "examples": [
            { "id": "rich-text-editor-images", "title": "Adding an image", "lead": "Choose the image tool, then Upload, or paste or drop an image into the text. Here images are embedded with `AllowDataImages`, so nothing is stored." }
          ]
        },
        {
          "id": "tables", "title": "Tables", "summary": "Insert tables, add and delete rows and columns, and give them a header row.", "members": ["RichTextTools"],
          "intro": [
            "The table tool is one menu. Insert table opens a dialog for the rows, the columns and a header row. With the caret in a table, the menu adds a row above or below or a column on either side, aligns the whole column, turns the header row on or off, or deletes a row, a column or the table. A new row keeps its column's alignment.",
            "A cell holds what the rest of the text can: formats, links, lists, images, and its own alignment from the alignment tools. A header row's cells are column headers.",
            "Tab and Shift+Tab move between cells. Tab in the last cell leaves the editor, so add a row from the menu."
          ],
          "examples": [
            { "id": "rich-text-editor-tables", "title": "A table of action items", "lead": "Put the caret in a cell and open the table menu to add a row or a column; Tab moves to the next cell." }
          ]
        },
        {
          "id": "task-lists", "title": "Task lists", "summary": "Lists of tasks with checkboxes, ticked as they're done.", "members": ["RichTextTools"],
          "intro": [
            "The task list tool (Ctrl+Shift+9) turns the selected lines into tasks, each with a checkbox in place of its bullet, and turns them back. Typing `[ ] ` or `[x] ` at the start of a line also starts a task, the second one already done.",
            "A click on a box checks it, and so does Ctrl+Enter with the caret in the task. Enter starts the next task, and Enter in an empty task ends the list. Tasks nest and indent like other list items.",
            "The value holds each task as GitHub-flavored Markdown renders it, `<li><input type=\"checkbox\" disabled checked>`, so task lists from GitHub or Markdig keep their boxes when they're pasted or bound. `RichText` shows the boxes read-only. In both, each box is named by its task's text for screen readers."
          ],
          "examples": [
            { "id": "rich-text-editor-tasks", "title": "A release checklist", "lead": "Tick a task, then see the same value in `RichText` below the editor." }
          ]
        },
        {
          "id": "code-blocks", "title": "Code blocks", "summary": "Mark a code block's language; RichText shows it highlighted.", "members": ["CodeLanguages"],
          "intro": [
            "With the caret in a code block, a button at its corner shows the block's language and opens a list of them. The value keeps the language as the code's class, `<pre><code class=\"language-csharp\">`, as Markdown, Prism and highlight.js write it. No colors or markup are saved.",
            "`RichText` highlights blocks with a language on the server, with the same highlighter as `Code`, so it works on static pages. HTML shown elsewhere keeps the class for the app's own highlighting.",
            "`CodeLanguages` sets the list, as language names such as `csharp`; it defaults to the languages the library's highlighter knows. The editor shows code plain while you write it."
          ],
          "examples": [
            { "id": "rich-text-editor-code-blocks", "title": "A code block with its language", "lead": "The editor marks the block as C#; `RichText` below it shows the same value highlighted." }
          ]
        },
        {
          "id": "styling", "title": "Styling the content", "summary": "The text uses Prose's styles, so it looks the same in the editor and where it's shown.", "members": ["FontSize", "ContentClass", "TopHeading"],
          "intro": [
            "The text is part of the page, not a frame, and has `Prose`'s styles: what you edit looks like what `RichText` shows. The theme's tokens reach it as they reach everything else. `FontSize` sets the text's size, on `Prose`'s scale. For rules of your own, give the editor a `ContentClass` and `RichText` the same `Class`; Prose's rules have the lowest weight, so yours win."
          ],
          "examples": [
            { "id": "rich-text-editor-content-style", "title": "A class on the content", "lead": "This help article is large text, and its headings are the primary color, in the editor and, below it, as `RichText` shows it." }
          ]
        }
      ]
