        { "feature": "A table of the values in a \"Show data\" disclosure" },
        { "feature": "Tooltips on hover and from the keyboard", "noscript": "none" },
        { "feature": "Legend items that hide and show a series", "noscript": "none" }
      ],
      "examples": [
        { "id": "chart-line-usage", "title": "A few lines over time", "lead": "`ChartLineSeries` in a `Chart` takes each item's `Value`. To write each line's name where it ends, so the reader doesn't look across to a legend, set `EndLabel`.", "after": ["With dates as the `Argument`, the points sit on a date scale, with a tick per month or year as the width allows.", "Keep to a few lines. Each takes the next color and a dash pattern and marker of its own, so they differ by more than color. With every line named, the chart leaves the legend out.", "Everything on the Chart page works with every series type."] },
        { "id": "chart-lines", "title": "Daily values and a moving average", "lead": "Daily numbers swing with the day of the week, which hides where they're heading. `Trend=\"ChartTrend.MovingAverage\"` with a `TrendPeriod` of 7 draws the average of each week beside the daily line, so the direction reads at a glance and the days stay in view. With this many points, the line has no markers." },
        { "id": "chart-line-smooth", "title": "Smooth lines", "lead": "`Curve=\"ChartCurve.Smooth\"` draws a curve through the values, which suits a measure that changes continuously between readings, such as temperature. The curve never overshoots a value, so it doesn't invent peaks the data doesn't have. Keep straight lines for counts and totals, where a curve suggests values between the points that were never measured." },
        { "id": "chart-line-step", "title": "Step lines", "lead": "`Curve=\"ChartCurve.Step\"` holds each value flat until the next one's date, then steps: for values that stay put until they change, such as a price, a rate or a plan's tier. Add a last item at today's date so the current value runs to the present." }
      ]
