      "examples": [
        { "id": "chart-bubble", "title": "Named bubbles for a third measure", "lead": "A bubble chart is a scatter whose circles are sized by a third measure. Use `ChartBubbleSeries` and give it a `Size`, here each product's revenue.", "after": ["Circles are sized by area, on one scale across every series: revenue twice as large is a circle twice the area. The largest is capped to the plot. Larger bubbles are drawn first, so none hides a smaller one.", "`SizeFormat` sets how sizes read in the tooltip and in Show data. With few bubbles, name each one with `LabelText`; names that would collide are left out at the widths where they would."] },
        { "id": "chart-bubble-world", "title": "Many bubbles, by group, on a log scale", "lead": "To spread out values that span a hundredfold, such as income, set `Logarithmic` on `ChartArgumentAxis`: each step of ten is an equal distance apart. Here each country's income is plotted against how long its people live, sized by population and colored by region with `SeriesBy`.", "after": ["With this many bubbles, not every name fits. List the items largest first, and `LabelText` names the biggest, leaving out those that would collide.", "`ShowSizeKey` adds a key to the legend, with circles for a round size near the largest and a quarter of it, so a size can be read and not only compared. `SizeFormat` sets how sizes read there. Here the legend is beside the chart, with `Legend=\"ChartLegend.End\"`."] }
