        { "id": "chart-candlestick", "title": "Candlesticks", "lead": "`ChartCandlestickSeries` takes each item's `Open`, `High`, `Low` and `Close`. A period that closed up is hollow and one that closed down is filled, so the direction shows without color.", "after": ["Dates under candles sit side by side by item, so weekends and holidays leave no gaps.", "`IncreaseColor` and `DecreaseColor` set the colors, such as green and red."] },
        { "id": "chart-candlestick-volume", "title": "With a moving average and volume", "lead": "A stock chart often adds a moving average over the candles and the volume traded beneath them. The 20-day average is a `ChartLineSeries` of a value worked out in the data, so it starts from the days before the chart's first. The volume is a bar chart of its own, joined to the prices with `Sync` for hover and zoom.", "after": ["A line series over the candles takes the same spacing by item; on a chart without bars or candles, `SkipGaps` on `ChartArgumentAxis` does it.", "The same `LabelWidth` on both value axes lines up their plots, and the prices' chart hides its dates with `HideLabels`, since the volume's show them. Only the prices' chart sets `Zoom`, so its buttons zoom both, and the volume follows.", "One title names the pair: the volume chart's `Title` is for screen readers, hidden with `HideTitle`, and each value axis's `Title` says what it measures. Both leave out their tables (`DataTable=\"ChartDataTable.None\"`), so nothing sits between the two plots."] }
